this is the second biggest fruit and veg  Market in South America  the grand Mercado mayorista in Lima is a  hub where much of Peru's produce comes  in and is then pumped out around the  capital and the rest of the country  it's a good barometer to check if Peru's  political crisis is affecting its food  supply  and it's starting to  less than half of the tomatoes that  usually arrive at running stalk are  coming in  usually there are 10 to 20 trucks and  these days they've been just three or  four of them  Market authorities say that many  products are unaffected but overall  deliveries are down 15 to 20 percent  roadblocks going up predominantly in the  country's South more than 50 of them  protesters want fresh elections and the  Congress replaced after the impeachment  imprisonment of ex-president Pedro  Castillo less produce arriving at the  market means higher prices that's  affecting especially poorer peruvians  like Sabina who we met buying at Ronald  stall  I came here early check the prices went  home to get my money came back and had  already gone up I can't afford it I've  got to buy food for five children with  five dollars and I just don't have  enough  United Nations say that half the  Peruvian population already lacks  regular access to sufficient nutrition  and a country suffering its highest  inflation in a decade  now things may worsen so you might think  Sabina would be against the roadblocks  not so  I suppose for the protesters because I'm  one of them I'm from rural Peru too  we're not being listened to we ask the  government for support and we're getting  nothing we're abandoned suffering with  drought and there's no help for her and  many others in the market this isn't  just about the current crisis it's about  a lima-centric political class that they  say doesn't care about those in the  countryside  their politicians have got into Congress  and not done anything for Peru just as  they got rid of the president who didn't  do any good either they should go too  Monica's potatoes a Cornerstone of  Peruvian cooking have gone up too it was  a struggle to get some of these spuds to  Market I asked her what she thought  would happen if the crisis continues  if the government doesn't start  resolving the problems engaging with the  population it's going to get out of  control and we could see looting because  if people in the Hills get too scared to  bring their produce to sale than those  in the capital will get desperate  we're not there yet the grand Mercado is  still well stocked for the political  crisis which ignited all this shows no  sign of subsiding  John Holman Al Jazeera Lima
 

